sec.p:
To determine variants that changed polysome distribution, we used read counts in polysome fractions to build a negative binomial generalized linear model with a log link function for each pair of oligonucleotides as follows:
ln⁡(Ci)=β0+βFFi+βEEi+βGGi+θGiFi+ηGiE \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \ln \left(C_{i}\right)=\beta_{0}+\beta_{F} F_{i}+\beta_{E} E_{i}+\beta_{G} G_{i}+\theta G_{i} F_{i}+\eta G_{i} E$$\end{document}
where Ci are read count values of the ith replicate, Fi represents the three polysome fractions (Mono, Light and Heavy), Ei represents biological replicates, and Gi represents WT or mutant.

To develop a prediction model for determining whether a genetic variant affects mRNA translation, we employed a logistic regression model with elastic net regularization for feature selection. The curated features included free energy, structure rate, GC content, k-mer, G-quadruplex (RG4), conservation level, miRNA binding motif, PUM motif, RBP motif, and ARE. Before feature selection, the features were narrowed down using multiple logistic regression models to regress the significance of variants (Yes/No) on each feature, with a p-value < 0.01 serving as the criterion for identifying potential candidates. Subsequently, the dataset was divided into two groups, comprising training and testing sets in a 2:1 ratio, respectively. The model is represented as follows:
ln⁡(P(Yi=1)1−P(Yi=1))=β0+∑j=1pβjXj \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \ln \left(\frac{\mathrm{P}\left(Y_{i}=1\right)}{1-\mathrm{P}\left(Y_{i}=1\right)}\right)=\beta_{0}+\sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_{j} X_{j}$$\end{document}
where Yi = 1 is the ith mt sequence that significantly affects mRNA translation, and Xji is the feature j in the ith sequence. The predictive features were standardized using Z-score transformation to achieve unit standard deviation, thereby ensuring comparability of coefficients. Elastic net regularization minimizes Llog+λ(1−α2∑j=1pβj2+α∑j=1p|βj|)\begin{document}$\mathrm{L}_{\log }+\lambda\left(\frac{1-\alpha}{2} \sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_{j}^{2}+\alpha \sum_{j=1}^{p}\left|\beta_{j}\right|\right)$\end{document}, where the negative log likelihood is:
Llog=−∑i=1n[−ln⁡(1+e(β0+∑j=1pβjXji))+yi(β0+∑j=1pβjXji) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle L_{\log }=-\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left[-\ln \left(1+e^{\left(\beta_{0}+\sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_{j} X_{j i}\right)}\right)+y_{i}\left(\beta_{0}+\sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_{j} X_{j i}\right)\right.$$\end{document}
We applied 10-fold cross-validation on the training set to optimize the tuning parameters (λ and α) for the elastic net model. Following this, predictions were made on the testing set to evaluate the model’s performance, and the area under the receiver operating characteristic (AUROC) was calculated. Feature selection and AUROC calculations were conducted using the R packages ‘caret’ (v.6.0.94) and ‘precrec’ (v.0.14.4) (Kuhn, 2008; Saito & Rehmsmeier, 2017), respectively, with all analyses performed on R version 4.3.3. The 95% confidence intervals for the AUROC values were estimated through bootstrap resampling with 1,000 iterations.

LASSO regression was performed on the representative features, with the sequence weights of Equation 2,

(3)

β(λ)=arg⁡minβ0,β{∑i=1nwi(TEi−β0−∑j=1pβjXij)2+λ∑j=1p|βj|}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \beta^{(\lambda)}=\underset{\beta_{0}, \beta}{\arg \min }\left\{\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_{i}\left(T E_{i}-\beta_{0}-\sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_{j} X_{i j}\right)^{2}+\lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p}\left|\beta_{j}\right|\right\} \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



where Xij is the standardised value of representative feature j for sequence i, p the number of representatives and n the number of sequences. λ was chosen by ten-fold cross-validation, as the value giving the smallest mean cross-validated error.
